#!/usr/bin/env python3
# SPDX-FileCopyrightText: Mariotaku <git@mariotaku.me> and the Beanjar contributors
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
"""Regression run over every supported firmware target (docs/dev/guides/build.md, "Regression runs"):

  scripts/regress (--host HOST | --local) [--target TARGET]... [--fw NAME]... [--headless] [--tv]
                  [--no-build] [--no-sync] [--keep-going] [--timeout S] [--settle S] [--run NAME]
                  [--dry-run] [-j N] [--viewer] [--tv-steps LIST] [--decode-cases LIST]

Drives the VM host over ssh from this machine (--host HOST, or REGRESS_HOST in the environment: an
ssh destination, with no built-in default; the checkout there is the directory DEST, default
beanjar, relative to its home), or this machine itself with --local. First, here:
`FW=<fw> scripts/build.sh guest` for each target's firmware (--no-build: only checks that the
published kernel and the target's layers exist), then the command named by REGRESS_SYNC (an
executable that copies this checkout to the VM host; it gets HOST and DEST in its environment) and
`scripts/build.sh host` on the VM host (--no-sync skips both: sync and build the host yourself). The
VM host boots each target's firmware from its own firmware collections (`host/scripts/vm-launcher config
FIRMWARE_DIRS` there), by the name chosen here; nothing is copied to it, and a target whose firmware
is not there fails without booting.
Then, strictly one target at a time, the checkout's development VM of the target (INSTANCE=<target>: a
development VM is bound to one firmware; never a library VM, and nothing starts while any VM,
mitmweb, slirp4netns or viewer runs on the VM host):
  start      FW=<fw> STATE=none LAYERS=normal,gl DISPLAY_BACKEND=dbus BUILD=0 host/scripts/vm-launcher start
             (NET_LINK and NET_MODE as the environment gives them, nat and direct by default; LAYERS,
             DISPLAY_BACKEND, NET_LINK, NET_MODE, AUDIO,
             ACCEL, PMLOG_DEBUG, MEM, SMP, KERNEL_ARGS, QEMU_EXTRA and GPU_DEVICE pass through; those of them not
             given are passed empty, the launcher's defaults, never what the VM's vm.toml kept)
  boot       tests/regress/guest.sh boot: bootd's boot-done, the guest's uptime at it
  check-m1   guest/scripts/check-m1.sh ("M1 PASS")
  app-calls  tests/regress/tvservice/app-calls.sh ("APP-CALLS PASS")
  checklist  the bring-up checklist: tests/regress/guest.sh checklist gathers the facts, and
             tests/regress/lib/checklist.py judges each item PASS, WARN or FAIL here (a WARN does not
             fail the target); the items' verdicts go into the summary and summary.json
  shot       once no Home launcher shows by itself (guest.sh home-idle: webOS 3.5's Home Auto
             Launch), HOME on the remote (tests/regress/ir-key.py), SETTLE s, tests/regress/virgl/dbus-grab.py (the
             screen: the UI over a black video plane); a Home that stays (webOS 4.5) gets HOME twice
  standby    Quick Start+ (docs/dev/architecture/suspend.md), where the firmware and the model offer it
             (tvpowerd opens /dev/lgib, tvservice's instantBooting; SKIP elsewhere): guest.sh standby-prep turns it on; the remote's POWER
             (ir-key.py 0x08); tests/regress/standby.py waits for the standby (the guest's
             webos.standby port), measures QEMU's CPU in it from STANDBY_SETTLE s on (the vCPUs
             idle in s2idle: under STANDBY_CPU %), presses the power key (QMP system_powerdown) and waits for the wake;
             guest.sh resumed: tvpowerd Active, the same tvservice and tvpowerd answering, the
             resume's time; then HOME as for shot and tests/regress/virgl/dbus-grab.py (<target>-resume.png)
  stop       host/scripts/vm-launcher stop, the guest's logs saved; device-census.json summarizes one
             completed snapshot's request counters and explicit failed opens (evidence, not a verdict);
             then no qemu, mitmweb, slirp4netns, viewer
             or regress unit (beanjar-vm-regress-*, in beanjar-regress.slice) may be left (a leftover
             ends the run whatever --keep-going says)
  capture    NET_LINK=off only: after stop, validate this target's fresh NIC pcap and check it for LG
             contacts; save net.pcap and pcap-lg.txt in the target's result directory
--headless, a system sanity run without graphics (on KVM, or TCG with --local on x86):
  start      FW=<fw> STATE=none LAYERS=normal,headless DISPLAY_BACKEND=none BUILD=0 host/scripts/vm-launcher start
  ready      tests/regress/guest.sh ready: core-boot-done made by tvservice's tv-ready (bootd's
             log has the generateSignal), not by bootd's timeout, which fails at once; the guest's
             uptime at it (a firmware with no TV service, N21D: core-boot-done itself). Headless
             boot-done waits on the first app and the CPU-idle check, so
             it is left to check-m1, which waits up to 150 s for it
  check-m1   as above (its gl checks skip: no gl layer)
  app-calls  as above
  tvservice  tests/regress/guest.sh tvservice: the same tvservice process as at ready, answering
  checklist  as above
  standby    as above, with tvpower's powerOff (reason remoteKey, guest.sh power-off) for POWER
             (headless has no compositor to take the key) and no screenshot
  stop       as above
  No HOME, no screenshot.
--headless runs -j N targets at once (--local: default 2, each SMP 4 vCPUs; on a VM host default 2, capped by its CPUs at two vCPUs each; capped by the CPUs at SMP vCPUs per VM, by
the targets' RAM (MEM, else each model's, nvm.board_ram: the largest ones running together must fit in the host's
MemAvailable, read at the start, less HEADROOM_MB = 1 GiB), and by the targets), each in an instance of its own, INSTANCE=<target>
(launch.instance_env: build/instances/<target>/ for its runtime files, logs, shares and state, the unit
beanjar-vm-regress-<target> in beanjar-regress.slice, its own slot and so its own host address, 127.0.1.<slot>, where its ports are at
their stock numbers: `host/scripts/vm-launcher config ADDRESS`), with NET_MODE=direct by default (QEMU's private slirp;
NET_MODE=mitm too, its inspector on the instance's address) and each target's model's RAM (nvm.board_ram; MEM overrides it). A failure (without --keep-going) or a leftover starts no further target; each target's leftover
check is its instance's, and the host as a whole is checked after the run. Every target's host/ has
meminfo-{start,ready,end}.txt (the host's free -m and /proc/meminfo).
--tv, the TV feature suite (tests/regress/lib/tvsuite.py; docs/dev/guides/build.md, "TV feature suite") instead of the
boot checks, in either mode, with NET_LINK=nat NET_MODE=direct (another network is a usage error):
  start      as above
  boot|ready as above (graphics: boot-done; headless: core-boot-done from tv-ready)
  luna-info  tests/regress/guest.sh luna-info runs tests/regress/tv.sh in webOS, which calls the public
             Luna APIs LG documents for apps (getSystemInfo's keys, getSystemSettings' option and
             caption keys and localeInfo, connectionmanager getStatus under both names,
             deviceid/getIDs, time/getSystemTime, getAppLoadStatus, mrcu, camera and blegatt
             getters), tvservice's identity keys and getCurrentCountryGroup, and every key of
             nyx-cmd OSInfo and DeviceInfo, read-only; tvsuite.judge_luna checks each answer's
             documented fields and its value against the target's board (board.cmdline's model,
             serial, MAC, chip, RAM), firmware (name: version, OTA id; release) and broadcast standard
  network    tests/regress/http-token.py serves a random token for the step in the VM's own network
             namespace (vm-launcher netns-exec), at 10.0.2.254 there; guest.sh network: eth0's lease and routes, the connection
             manager's status, a name through the guest's resolver, the token fetched with wget;
             tvsuite.judge_network: dhcp-lease, connman-wired, dns (against the VM host's own
             answer), http (the token, and the server's log of the request), online (connman's
             online state against what the VM host gets of the firmware's Ipv4StatusUrl)
  apps       each stock app of tests/regress/tv-expect's apps (Settings first), through guest.sh
             app-launch, app-state and app-close (tv.sh): sam's launch, in front, the remote's keys
             (ir-key.py), still in front in the same process, a screenshot (dbus-grab.py) not black,
             closed (an app needing terms the set has not agreed to, the expected table's eula from its
             requiredEULA: LG's terms screen that sam opens in its place, the same way); the
             test web app (tests/regress/tvapp, ares-package'd here into the run
             directory) installed with dev/install (graphics LAYERS add devmode) and the same.
             No crash report of the app's process from launch to close. Headless: every app
             launched, no keys or screenshot; none can stay up there (no WebAppMgr, no compositor,
             the input apps' stand-in ends), and each must be gone after a pause, sam answering
  tee        where the firmware has a TEE client (the expected table's tee; else SKIP, citing that no ELF
             of its paks names /dev/tee): guest.sh tee runs tests/regress/tee/tee-probe in webOS through the
             firmware's own libteec: /dev/tee0, the secure-store TA's session and each shipped
             TA's (ITEM_NOT_FOUND by design), the secure-store commands with their round trips,
             checked against the VM's identity/tee-huk (tests/regress/lib/teeref.py), and no crash
             report of a TEE client since the boot (tvsuite.judge_tee)
  draw       graphics: the test app's pattern with canvas 2D and with WebGL, judged on the
             screenshot (tvsuite.judge_draw)
  audio      QEMU records the card on the VM host (tests/regress/audio/capture.py: HMP wavcapture on its
             audiodev) while guest.sh audio plays a tone per phase with paplay: the set's volume,
             five volumeDown, setMuted, unmuted and five volumeUp, each read back with the Audio
             API's getVolume (the expected table's audio); at the set's volume also one with aplay to
             ALSA's default (the pulse plugin) and one with aplay to pcm_output's hw PCM (heard
             only: it bypasses PulseAudio); graphics: the test app's WebAudio tone too;
             tvsuite.tone_facts measures each tone's level on the VM host, tvsuite.judge_audio
             judges
  decode     graphics: the test app (its copy in the run directory, with the clips
             tests/regress/video/make-clips.sh --decode makes here) plays each case of tvsuite.DECODE_CASES
             (H.264+AAC, HEVC+AAC, VP9, MPEG-2+AAC in TS, VP9+Opus and AV1+Opus in WebM with <video>;
             H.264+AAC, HEVC+AAC, VP9, VP9+Opus and AV1+Opus with its Media Source player) that the expected table's decode row expects (else SKIP, citing it),
             while a viewer of the case's own decodes what the guest's sinks send (headless, or the
             --viewer window: the one judged), recording its log, a capture a second and the media
             audio (never played); tvsuite.media_facts on the VM host, tvsuite.judge_decode here:
             the page's playing, currentTime and ended, the host's decoder, pictures shown and their
             pts, end of stream, captures with a changing picture at the window, filling the page's
             video rect whole, the audio's level.
             Headless: SKIP. Then dev/remove of the test app
  smp        graphics: the SMP check app (tests/regress/smp/pack.sh here, with build/tests/regress/smp/smpcheck and an
             8 s H.264 clip) installed with dev/install, launched by sam (guest.sh smp): a
             StarfishMediaAPIs client as ss4s's player, which feeds the clip and logs every
             callback; the viewer decodes what the sink sends; tvsuite.judge_smp: FRAMEREADY
             while it plays, advancing to the clip's end, the clip decoded on the host. Then
             dev/remove. Headless, or a firmware whose library lacks a symbol the app looks up
             (the expected table's smp row, tvsuite.smp_plan): SKIP
  stop       as above
  Each check is PASS or FAIL, or SKIP where tests/regress/tv-expect/<target>.toml (tests/regress/tv-expect-gen.py,
  from the firmware) has the API, method or key absent from the target's firmware, citing the
  evidence; no WARN. A step fails if any of its checks fails. The table has each step's verdict
  and its PASS/FAIL/SKIP counts, then every FAIL; summary.json has every check under "tv".
  --tv-steps LIST runs only those of the lane's steps. The apps' screenshots and facts are in each
  target's apps/, and gallery.html in the run directory shows them.
A graphics run (LAYERS with gl) skips a target whose build makes no gl layer, as guest/layers/gl/meson.build
decides from the chip's table: a guest/layers/soc/<chip>/meson.build with neither gl_vendor_stubs nor
gl_vendor_gl (no GPU vendor stand-ins). It neither builds nor boots it; its row says SKIP. A target
that should have one and has none after the build (or with --no-build) fails the run.
A guest restart ends the step that waits on it and fails the target, except the one restart of a
target whose firmware restarts the set on the first boot of a fresh state (its file's
first_boot_restart): follow_restart waits for the unit's new QEMU and runs the steps again from the boot.
Each step has TIMEOUT seconds (default 300). Under TCG (--local on x86, or ACCEL=tcg) every deadline
of the run's own (TIMEOUT, check-m1's boot-done wait, the standby's waits) is stretched by the
launcher's TCG factor, qemu.TIME_SCALE, as the guest's bootd timers are; under KVM it is 1. Results, on the VM host and copied back here:
  build/regress/<run>/<target>.png      the home screen (opaque RGB, as a TV shows it)
  build/regress/<run>/<target>/         <step>.txt and .rc (the guest's), start/stop/shot/orphans.txt,
                                      saved/ (the logs saved at stop and check-m1's), host/ (the
                                      launcher's console.log, webos.log, qemu.out, network logs)
  build/regress/<run>/summary.txt     the table printed at the end (and summary.json), the mode
                                      (graphics or headless) on each target's row
The targets are targets.MODELS' (`host/scripts/vm-launcher config TARGETS`); a target whose model is in targets.BRING_UP is still being
brought up and is left out without --target (named, it runs), and so is a target that has never booted (its
file's tested = false: targets.untested_targets, warned about when named); a target's firmware is the one in the host's
firmware collections (beanjar host config's firmware_dirs) that resolves to it (paths.firmware_target: its model code and the
release its rootfs names; the default firmware first; --fw NAME picks one where there are several). Exits 1 if any target failed, 2 on a usage error.
"""

